WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.420
One of the most powerful ideas in deep learning is that sometimes you can take knowledge that

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:10.720
the neural network has learned from one task and apply that knowledge to a separate task.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.180
So for example, maybe you can have a neural network learn to recognize objects like cats

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.540
and then use that knowledge or use part of that knowledge to help you do a better job

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.480
reading x-ray scans.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.540
This is called transfer learning.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.420
Let's take a look.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:30.800
Let's say you've trained a neural network on image recognition.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.140
So you first take a neural network and train it on x-y pairs where x is an image and y

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.100
is some object in the image.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.500
It's a cat or a dog or a bird or something else.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.420
If you want to take this neural network and adapt or we say transfer what is learned to

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:55.180
a different task such as radiology diagnosis, meaning really reading x-ray scans, where you

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.940
can do is take this loss output layer of the neural network and just delete that and delete

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:07.180
also the waste feeding into that loss output layer and create a new set of randomly initialized

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:15.180
waste just for the loss layer and have that now output radiology diagnosis.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.620
So to be concrete, during the first phase of training where you're training on an image

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:23.300
recognition task, you train all of the usual parameters for the neural network, all

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:33.080
the ways, all the layers, and you have something that now learns to make image recognition predictions.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:39.040
Having trained that neural network, what you now do to implement transfer learning is swap

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:50.040
in a new data set x-y where now these are radiology images and y are the diagnoses

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.440
you want to predict.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.800
And what you do is initialize the loss layer's waste.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:06.800
Let's call that WL and BL randomly and now retrain the neural network on this new data

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.720
set, on the new radiology data set.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.520
You have a couple options of how you retrain the neural network with radiology data.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.520
You might, if you have a small radiology data set, you might want to just retrain the

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.800
waste of the loss layer, just WL, BL, and keep the rest of the parameters fixed.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.920
If you have enough data, you could also retrain all the layers of the rest of the neural network.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.720
And the rule of thumb is maybe if you have a small data set, then just retrain the one

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.240
loss layer, the output layer, or maybe the loss one or two layers.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:41.800
But if you have a lot of data, then maybe you can retrain all the parameters in the

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:42.800
network.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.320
And if you retrain all the parameters in the neural network, then this initial phase of

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:55.600
training on image recognition is sometimes called pre-training because you're using image

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.960
recognition's data to pre-initialize or really pre-train the waste of the neural network.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:07.080
And then if you're updating all the ways afterward, then training on the radiology data, sometimes

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.000
that's called fine tuning.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.320
So if you hear the words pre-training and fine tuning in a deep learning context, this

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.480
is what they mean when they refer to pre-training and fine tuning weights in a transfer learning

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.480
task.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:27.000
And what you've done in this example is you've taken knowledge, learned from image recognition,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.400
and applied it or transferred it to radiology diagnosis.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.880
And the reason this can be helpful is that a lot of the low-level features such as detecting

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:41.160
edges, detecting curves, detecting parts of objects, learning from that, from a very

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:46.960
large image recognition data set might help your learning algorithm do better in radiology

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:47.960
diagnosis.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:53.080
It's just learned a lot about the structure and the nature of how images look like, and

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.600
some of that knowledge will be useful.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:01.600
So having learned to recognize images, it might have learned enough about just what

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.040
parts of different images look like, that knowledge about lines, dots, curves, and

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.720
so on, maybe small parts of objects, that knowledge could help your radiology diagnosis

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.120
network learn a bit faster or learn with less data.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:17.760
Here's another example.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.600
Let's say that you've trained a speech recognition system.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:27.760
So now X is inputs of audio or audio snippets, and Y is some transcript.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:34.520
So you trained in speech recognition system to output your transcripts.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:45.610
And let's say that you now want to build a wake word or a trigger word detection system.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:50.570
So recall that the wake word or the trigger word are the words we say in order to wake

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:55.450
up speech-controlled devices in a whole set, such as saying Alexa to wake up an Amazon

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:00.490
Echo or OK Google to wake up a Google device or Hey Siri to wake up an Apple device or

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.690
saying, Ni hao, bai du to wake up a bai du device.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:10.410
So in order to do this, you might take out the last layer of the neural network again

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.650
and create a new output node.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:19.170
But sometimes another thing you could do is actually create not just a single new output,

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:24.570
but actually create several new layers to your neural network to try to predict the

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:27.370
labels Y for your wake word detection problem.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:32.850
And again, depending on how much data you have, you might just retrain the new layers

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:39.090
of the network or maybe you could retrain even more layers of this neural network.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.650
So when does transfer learning make sense?

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:47.330
Transfer learning makes sense when you have a lot of data for the problem you're transferring

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:52.530
from and usually relatively less data for the problem you're transferring to.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:58.130
So for example, let's say you have a million examples for image recognition tasks.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:03.610
So that's a lot of data to learn a lot of low level features or to learn a lot of useful

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:06.730
features in the earlier layers of the neural network.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:12.170
But for the radiology task, maybe you have only 100 examples.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:15.530
So you have very little data for the radiology diagnosis problem.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:17.870
Maybe you have only 100 X-ray scans.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:23.170
So a lot of knowledge you learn from image recognition can be transferred and can really

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:29.890
help you get going with radiology recognition, even if you don't have a lot of data for radiology.

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:34.170
For speech recognition, maybe you've trained the speech recognition system on 10,000 hours

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.290
of data.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:40.090
So you've learned a lot about what human voices sounds like from the 10,000 hours of data,

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:41.770
which really is a lot.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:46.090
But for your trigger word detection, maybe you have only one hour of data.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.890
So that's not a lot of data to fit a lot of parameters.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:55.010
So in this case, a lot of what you learn about what human voices sound like, what are components

00:06:55.010 --> 00:07:00.370
of human speech and so on, that can be really helpful for building a good wake word detector,

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:04.690
even though you have a relatively small data set, or at least a much smaller data set for

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:08.210
the wake word detection task.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:12.490
So in both of these cases, you're transferring from a problem with a lot of data to a

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:15.930
problem with relatively little data.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:22.490
One case where transfer learning would not make sense is if the opposite was true.

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:29.290
So if you had a hundred images for image recognition, and you had a hundred images for radiology

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:35.130
diagnosis, or even your thousand images for radiology diagnosis, one way to think about

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:40.050
it is that to do well on radiology diagnosis, assuming what you really want to do well

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:45.570
on is radiology diagnosis, having radiology images is much more valuable than having

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.770
cats and dog and so on images.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:52.650
So each example here is much more valuable than each example there, at least for the

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:56.050
purpose of building a good radiology system.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:01.050
So if you already have more data for radiology, it's not that likely that having a hundred

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:07.130
images of your random objects of cats and dogs and cars and so on, would be that helpful.

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:11.970
Because the value of one example of image from your image recognition task of cats

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:17.730
and dogs is just less valuable than one example of an x-ray image for the task of building

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.090
a good radiology system.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.370
So this would be one example with transfer learning.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:27.650
Well, it might not hurt, but I wouldn't expect it to give you any meaningful gain either.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:32.650
And similarly, if you built a speech recognition system on 10 hours of data, and you actually

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:38.570
have 10 hours or maybe even more, say 50 hours of data for wake word detection, you

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:43.010
know, it may or may not hurt, maybe it won't hurt to include that 10 hours of data to do

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:47.490
transfer learning, but you just wouldn't expect to get a meaningful gain.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:51.010
So to summarize, when does transfer learning make sense?

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:59.820
If you're trying to learn from some task A and transfer some of the knowledge to some

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:06.820
task B, then transfer learning makes sense when tasks A and B have the same input x.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:12.460
In the first example, A and B both had images as input.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:17.500
In the second example, both had audio clips as input.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:22.620
It tends to make sense when you have a lot more data for task A than task B.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:28.180
All this is under the assumption that what you really want to do well on is task B.

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:33.980
And because data for task B is more valuable for task B, usually you just need a lot more

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:40.780
data for task A because each example from task A is just less valuable for task B than

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:43.780
each example for task B.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:48.260
And then finally, transfer learning will tend to make more sense if you suspect that low-level

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:52.540
features from task A could be helpful for learning task B.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:56.980
And in both of the earlier examples, maybe learning image recognition teaches you about

00:09:56.980 --> 00:10:01.900
images to help with radiology diagnosis, and maybe learning speech recognition teaches you

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:06.100
about human speech to help you with trigger word or wake word detection.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:10.060
So to summarize, transfer learning tends to be most useful if you're trying to do well

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:14.660
on some task B, usually a problem where you have relatively little data.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:19.940
So for example, in radiology, it's difficult to get that many x-ray scans to build a good

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.580
radiology diagnosis system.

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:26.380
So in that case, you might find a related but different task such as image recognition,

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:30.460
where you can get maybe a million images and learn a lot of low-level features from

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:35.860
that so that you can then try to do well on task B, on your radiology task, despite

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.220
not having that much data for it.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:42.180
When transfer learning makes sense, it does help the performance of your learning

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:43.180
algorithm significantly.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:49.060
But I've also sometimes seen transfer learning applied in settings where task A actually

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:53.980
has less data than task B, and in those cases, you kind of don't expect to see

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:55.580
much of a gain.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:59.500
So that's it for transfer learning, where you learn from one task and try to transfer

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:00.500
to a different task.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:04.660
There's another version of learning from multiple tasks, which is called multi-task

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:08.580
learning, which is when you try to learn from multiple tasks at the same time rather than

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:13.380
learning from one and then sequentially or after that, trying to transfer to a different

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:14.380
task.

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:17.140
So in the next video, let's discuss multi-task learning.
